  "query_preview": "/TUESDAY THROUGH SATURDAY/...\nIssued at 304 AM EDT Sun Sep 27 2026\n\nTuesday - Wednesday Night\n\nThe surface high pressure will begin to shift eastward on Tuesday \nwhich in turn brings a southerly flow back into the region. By \nWednesday afternoon, flow aloft will be out of the southwest \nbringing warmer temperatures and moisture. Maximum temperatures are \nexpected to warm into mid to upper 80s Wednesday afternoon. There \nis a chance of temperatures overachieving again due to deep mixing \nheights leading to dry air mix down. \n\nThursday - Saturday\n\nA low pressure system that develops over the Plains mid week will \ncontinue to move eastward. The associated weak ridging over the \nOhio Valley will then collide with a shortwave disturbance moving \nthrough the Great Lakes region. Rain chances will return Thursday \nevening/night ahead of an associated frontal boundary. The region \nwill then be set in a zonal flow aloft by late week, which will \nresult in lingering rain showers through the weekend. QPF totals \nthrough the end of the week are ranging from 1-1.5 inches across the \nCWA. By Sunday, guidance is showing signals of shortwave troughing \nthat will eject into the Ohio Valley, along with the jet stream \ndigging south into the region. This would lead to more rain chances \nand likely cooler temperatures if a strong enough cold front were to \npush through. There is low confidence in this system at this point.",
